<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stalinism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stalinism is the system of rule and the policies associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the late 1920s until 1953. It combined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and a personality cult with forced collectivisation of agriculture, rapid industrialisation through central planning, and mass terror by the secret police, including show trials and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. The term is also used for regimes elsewhere, such as Albania under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>, that copied the model.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stalinism.jpg" alt="Stalinism" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Stalin_in_1937_with_his_pipe_smoking.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…100 million victims is the estimate the Victims of Communism Memorial Foundation most commonly uses, though some of its own authors distanced themselves from it, helped legitimise comparisons of Nazism and <b>Stalinism</b>, and museums and monuments to the victims stand in Eastern Europe, Canada and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1961 the writer Ralph Giordano, who had followed <b>Stalinism</b> until about 1955, used the phrase as the title of a book settling accounts with it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lied-der-partei">Lied der Partei</a></li><li><blockquote>Mátyás Rákosi, leader of the Hungarian Working People's Party, ruled as a <b>Stalinist</b> dictator, with show trials such as that of László Rajk and a feared secret police, the ÁVH.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime was strictly <b>Stalinist</b>: land was collectivised, industry nationalised and the Sigurimi secret police watched everyone.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania">People's Socialist Republic of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>The promised autonomy was later revoked, and Ingrian Finns suffered <b>Stalinist</b> deportations.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-north-ingria">Republic of North Ingria</a></li><li><blockquote>Moscow had him removed and later executed, and from 1932 Salchak Toka, leader of the Tuvan People's Revolutionary Party, ran a <b>Stalinist</b> regime: herds were collectivised, monasteries closed and lamas persecuted.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuvan-peoples-republic">Tuvan People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
